"Compares category values using dots on a common quantitative axis without encoding magnitude as bar length.\n\nUse when: Small differences deserve a focused, explicitly labeled axis range. Many categories require a compact and accurate comparison.\n\nAvoid when: Readers require visible additive components or raw observation distributions.\n\nVisual grammar: One categorical position identifies each item. Dot position along the quantitative axis encodes its value.\n\nBuild: Choose one estimate per category and state its aggregation. Sort rows by value or a defensible domain order. Draw dots with short row labels and sparse reference guides.\n\nCheck: Check a narrowed axis includes clear numeric ticks. Verify equal values align exactly across rows. Confirm marker size stays constant unless another variable is explained.\n\nPitfalls: Connecting unrelated categories introduces a false continuity. A dot at the axis edge may conceal a clipped value.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic satisfaction averages for twelve services using a labeled 1–5 axis and aligned dots.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, Dumbbell chart, Forest plot."
